Phase One Sensor+ — attribution and scope of `--diamond`
--------------------------------------------------------

The `--diamond` option was inspired by a published Phase One white paper
(W. Borchenko, "Phase One Patent Pending Sensor+ Explained", 2009), which
identifies the diamond-shaped super-pixel as the fix for the aliasing that
conventional Bayer binning produces. That document is credited in section 2.4
of docs/rwmono-whitepaper.md as the source of the insight. It is not a source
of code or design: no Phase One software, specification or figure was used,
and no figure from that document is reproduced anywhere in this repository.

Sensor+ is a trademark of Phase One A/S. References to it here are descriptive
(nominative) use, identifying whose published work prompted the approach. This
project is not affiliated with, sponsored by, or endorsed by Phase One A/S.

### Patent awareness

Phase One holds a patent family on the *colour* binning method described in
that white paper:

    US 7,929,807 B2   filed 2007-02-27, expires approx. 2030-02-18
    EP 1,887,515 B1   granted 2011-12-28, expires 2027-02-27
    JP 4,805,294 B2   granted

Its independent claims are not limited to in-sensor processing — claim 1
expressly recites a method "performed on at least one of an image sensor, a
processing circuit, an external processing device, a processing device, or a
computer processor" — and claim 15 covers a computer program product. Software
implementations are therefore within the intended scope of the claims.

What claim 1 requires, and `--diamond` does not do:

  * generate a macro pixel from n x n *first* (red) pixels;
  * generate a macro pixel from n x n *fourth* (blue) pixels;
  * generate *separate* macro pixels for the second (Gr) and third (Gb) green
    planes, each from n x n photosites of a single green type, on two
    45-degree-rotated squares;
  * place the optical centres of the first, second and third macro pixels at
    identical relative positions so that they form a uniform grid.

`--diamond` is a monochrome operation. It discards red and blue entirely,
produces no colour mosaic and no uniform multi-plane optical-centre grid, and
its single green macro pixel averages two Gr and two Gb photosites together
rather than binning either green plane within itself. What remains — a
four-neighbour average on a quincunx lattice followed by decimation — is
long-established multirate sampling theory that predates the 2007 priority
date by decades.

### Constraint on future work

While the patents above remain in force, `--diamond` (or any successor) must
NOT be extended to emit a binned *colour* mosaic: no half-resolution RGGB
output, and no separate Gr/Gb macro pixels on their own grids. Either change
would track claim 1 element for element. Revisit after February 2030.

